<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The Tasks tab: a week of care, one day at a time.

    Top to bottom:
      - **The header** (monogram, "Tasks", theme toggle, pet count), shared with Home and Pets. Fixed:
        it is identity and controls, not content.
      - Everything else scrolls as one page:
          · the **week strip** — seven day cells, the chosen one a solid pill;
          · the **progress card** — the date, "5 of 6 done", "Reset day", a segmented bar and the
            gesture guide;
          · two **filter rows** — categories, then pets;
          · the **task list**, grouped under Morning / Afternoon / Evening;
          · the two **actions** — "+ Add task" and "Delegate".

    **This layout serves two screens.** Reached from the bottom bar it is the Tasks tab. Reached from a
    pet's detail page it is that pet's checklist: the fragment hides the header (the shared toolbar
    with its back arrow is shown instead — see MainActivity.updateChromeFor) and the pet row, and
    everything else is identical. See the note at the top of nav_graph.xml.

    **Why one NestedScrollView with the list inside it, rather than a RecyclerView for the page.** The
    design scrolls the week strip, card, chips and actions away together with the tasks. Those controls
    are a handful of stateful views, not rows, and forcing them into adapter items would make every
    chip tap an adapter update. A day's task list is short and bounded — the care scheduled for a
    household in one day — so the list is given wrap_content height with nestedScrollingEnabled="false",
    handing all scrolling to the page. The cost is that every card for the day is inflated at once
    rather than recycled; at the size of one day's care that is the right trade. The Pets tab, whose
    list has no such bound, is built the other way.

    **Why a CoordinatorLayout at the root.** This screen shows several Snackbars — "Breakfast deleted ·
    Undo", the reset confirmation, delegation results. A Snackbar attaches itself to the nearest
    CoordinatorLayout above the view it is given; without one here it would climb all the way to the
    window and appear on top of the bottom navigation bar, covering the tabs. Inside it, a vertical LinearLayout does the actual arrangement: the header takes the
    height it needs, and the scrolling page takes the rest through layout_height="0dp" with
    layout_weight="1".
-->
<androidx.coordinatorlayout.widget.CoordinatorLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    tools:context=".ui.checklist.ChecklistFragment">

<LinearLayout
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:orientation="vertical">

    <!-- The shared header, as a nested binding (`binding.header`). Hidden for a single pet. -->
    <include
        android:id="@+id/header"
        layout="@layout/view_tab_header" />

    <androidx.core.widget.NestedScrollView
        android:id="@+id/checklistScroll"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="0dp"
        android:layout_weight="1"
        android:clipToPadding="false"
        android:paddingBottom="@dimen/list_padding_bottom">

        <LinearLayout
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:orientation="vertical">

            <!-- ============================ Week strip ============================
                 Seven includes of one cell layout, each with its own id so ViewBinding gives the
                 fragment day0 … day6. Built in XML rather than added in code because the count is
                 fixed at seven: a static row is simpler to read and preview than one assembled at
                 runtime. Equal widths come from layout_weight; the gaps are start margins on all but
                 the first cell, so the outer cells line up with the screen gutter exactly. -->
            <LinearLayout
                android:id="@+id/weekStrip"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_xs"
                android:baselineAligned="false"
                android:orientation="horizontal"
                android:paddingStart="@dimen/screen_padding"
                android:paddingEnd="@dimen/screen_padding">

                <include
                    android:id="@+id/day0"
                    layout="@layout/item_week_day"
                    android:layout_width="0dp"
                    android:layout_height="@dimen/week_day_height"
                    android:layout_weight="1" />

                <include
                    android:id="@+id/day1"
                    layout="@layout/item_week_day"
                    android:layout_width="0dp"
                    android:layout_height="@dimen/week_day_height"
                    android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/week_day_gap"
                    android:layout_weight="1" />

                <include
                    android:id="@+id/day2"
                    layout="@layout/item_week_day"
                    android:layout_width="0dp"
                    android:layout_height="@dimen/week_day_height"
                    android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/week_day_gap"
                    android:layout_weight="1" />

                <include
                    android:id="@+id/day3"
                    layout="@layout/item_week_day"
                    android:layout_width="0dp"
                    android:layout_height="@dimen/week_day_height"
                    android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/week_day_gap"
                    android:layout_weight="1" />

                <include
                    android:id="@+id/day4"
                    layout="@layout/item_week_day"
                    android:layout_width="0dp"
                    android:layout_height="@dimen/week_day_height"
                    android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/week_day_gap"
                    android:layout_weight="1" />

                <include
                    android:id="@+id/day5"
                    layout="@layout/item_week_day"
                    android:layout_width="0dp"
                    android:layout_height="@dimen/week_day_height"
                    android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/week_day_gap"
                    android:layout_weight="1" />

                <include
                    android:id="@+id/day6"
                    layout="@layout/item_week_day"
                    android:layout_width="0dp"
                    android:layout_height="@dimen/week_day_height"
                    android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/week_day_gap"
                    android:layout_weight="1" />

            </LinearLayout>

            <!-- ============================ Progress card ============================
                 The one figure the user comes to check — how much of the day is done — on a light
                 surface card so it stands apart from the list. -->
            <com.google.android.material.card.MaterialCardView
                android:id="@+id/progressCard"
                style="@style/PetCare.Card.Feature"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/screen_padding"
                android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_md"
                android:layout_marginEnd="@dimen/screen_padding"
                app:shapeAppearanceOverlay="@style/ShapeAppearance.PetCare.XLarge">

                <LinearLayout
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:orientation="vertical"
                    android:padding="@dimen/card_padding">

                    <!-- The date and the count on the left, "Reset day" on the right, vertically
                         centred against the two lines of text. -->
                    <LinearLayout
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:baselineAligned="false"
                        android:gravity="center_vertical"
                        android:orientation="horizontal">

                        <LinearLayout
                            android:layout_width="0dp"
                            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                            android:layout_weight="1"
                            android:orientation="vertical">

                            <!-- "THURSDAY 11 SEPTEMBER" — the section-label treatment, so it labels
                                 the count beneath rather than competing with it. -->
                            <TextView
                                android:id="@+id/progressDateText"
                                style="@style/PetCare.SectionLabel"
                                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                                tools:text="Thursday 11 September" />

                            <TextView
                                android:id="@+id/progressCountText"
                                style="@style/PetCare.Headline"
                                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                                android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_xs"
                                android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceHeadlineMedium"
                                android:textColor="?attr/colorOnSurface"
                                tools:text="5 of 6 done" />

                        </LinearLayout>

                        <!-- Clears the ticks shown on screen — the same action as shaking the
                             phone, and it asks the same confirmation first. -->
                        <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
                            android:id="@+id/resetDayButton"
                            style="@style/PetCare.SmallPillButton"
                            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                            android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/spacing_sm"
                            android:text="@string/tasks_reset_day" />

                    </LinearLayout>

                    <!-- One segment per task; drawn by ui/common/SegmentedProgressView.kt. Marked
                         unimportant for accessibility because a drawing has nothing to read aloud —
                         the fragment gives the count above a full "5 of 6 tasks done" description
                         instead, so the bar's information is still spoken once. -->
                    <com.prash.petcare.ui.common.SegmentedProgressView
                        android:id="@+id/progressSegments"
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_md"
                        android:importantForAccessibility="no" />

                    <TextView
                        android:id="@+id/progressHelpText"
                        style="@style/PetCare.Subtitle"
                        android:layout_width="match_parent"
                        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                        android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_md"
                        android:textAppearance="?attr/textAppearanceBodySmall"
                        tools:text="@string/tasks_progress_help" />

                </LinearLayout>

            </com.google.android.material.card.MaterialCardView>

            <!-- ============================ Filter rows ============================
                 Each a single line of chips that slides sideways. A ChipGroup wraps onto more lines by
                 default; singleLine inside a HorizontalScrollView keeps each row one chip tall however
                 many options there are. clipToPadding="false" lets the first chip sit in the screen
                 gutter while the row still scrolls past the edge.

                 singleSelection: one option at a time. selectionRequired: tapping the chosen chip
                 cannot un-choose it, which would leave the row with nothing selected. -->
            <HorizontalScrollView
                android:id="@+id/categoryChipScroll"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_md"
                android:clipToPadding="false"
                android:paddingStart="@dimen/screen_padding"
                android:paddingEnd="@dimen/screen_padding"
                android:scrollbars="none">

                <com.google.android.material.chip.ChipGroup
                    android:id="@+id/categoryChipGroup"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    app:chipSpacingHorizontal="@dimen/pill_spacing"
                    app:selectionRequired="true"
                    app:singleLine="true"
                    app:singleSelection="true" />

            </HorizontalScrollView>

            <!-- The pet row. Hidden for a single pet's checklist, where there is nothing to choose. -->
            <HorizontalScrollView
                android:id="@+id/petChipScroll"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_xs"
                android:clipToPadding="false"
                android:paddingStart="@dimen/screen_padding"
                android:paddingEnd="@dimen/screen_padding"
                android:scrollbars="none">

                <com.google.android.material.chip.ChipGroup
                    android:id="@+id/petChipGroup"
                    android:layout_width="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    app:chipSpacingHorizontal="@dimen/pill_spacing"
                    app:selectionRequired="true"
                    app:singleLine="true"
                    app:singleSelection="true" />

            </HorizontalScrollView>

            <!-- ============================== Task list ==============================
                 Section headers and task cards in one RecyclerView — see ChecklistAdapter. Given
                 wrap_content and no nested scrolling, so the page above scrolls it; see the note at
                 the top of this file. -->
            <androidx.recyclerview.widget.RecyclerView
                android:id="@+id/checklistRecyclerView"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:nestedScrollingEnabled="false"
                android:paddingStart="@dimen/screen_padding"
                android:paddingEnd="@dimen/screen_padding"
                tools:itemCount="3"
                tools:listitem="@layout/item_checklist_task" />

            <!-- Shown in the list's place when there is nothing to show. The wording is set in code,
                 because "no care matches this day" and "you have no pets" are different situations
                 that both leave the list empty. -->
            <LinearLayout
                android:id="@+id/emptyChecklistContainer"
                style="@style/PetCare.EmptyState"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_xl"
                android:layout_marginBottom="@dimen/spacing_lg"
                android:visibility="gone"
                tools:visibility="gone">

                <ImageView
                    android:id="@+id/emptyChecklistIcon"
                    style="@style/PetCare.EmptyStateIcon"
                    android:contentDescription="@null"
                    android:src="@drawable/ic_empty_tasks" />

                <TextView
                    android:id="@+id/emptyChecklistTitle"
                    style="@style/PetCare.EmptyStateTitle"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:text="@string/tasks_empty_title" />

                <TextView
                    android:id="@+id/emptyChecklistMessage"
                    style="@style/PetCare.EmptyStateMessage"
                    android:layout_width="match_parent"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:text="@string/tasks_empty_message" />

            </LinearLayout>

            <!-- ============================== Actions ==============================
                 Two equal buttons: outlined "+ Add task" and filled "Delegate". Equal widths through
                 layout_weight on zero-width children, so the row is balanced whatever the labels'
                 lengths in another language. They replace the floating Delegate button this screen
                 used to have, which covered the bottom of the last card. -->
            <LinearLayout
                android:id="@+id/actionsRow"
                android:layout_width="match_parent"
                android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                android:layout_marginTop="@dimen/spacing_sm"
                android:baselineAligned="false"
                android:orientation="horizontal"
                android:paddingStart="@dimen/screen_padding"
                android:paddingEnd="@dimen/screen_padding">

                <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
                    android:id="@+id/addTaskButton"
                    style="@style/PetCare.TaskActionButton.Outlined"
                    android:layout_width="0dp"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_weight="1"
                    android:text="@string/tasks_add_task" />

                <!-- Hands the outstanding tasks to someone else by text or email. Disabled, not
                     hidden, when nothing is left — a control that vanishes leaves the user wondering
                     where it went. -->
                <com.google.android.material.button.MaterialButton
                    android:id="@+id/delegateButton"
                    style="@style/PetCare.TaskActionButton.Filled"
                    android:layout_width="0dp"
                    android:layout_height="wrap_content"
                    android:layout_marginStart="@dimen/spacing_sm"
                    android:layout_weight="1"
                    android:text="@string/checklist_delegate" />

            </LinearLayout>

        </LinearLayout>

    </androidx.core.widget.NestedScrollView>

</LinearLayout>

</androidx.coordinatorlayout.widget.CoordinatorLayout>
